    <heading level="1">6.2. Self-Check Exercises</heading>
    <exercise number="1" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Find $$\int{\sqrt{2x + 5}\mathit{dx}}$$ .</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>Let $$u = 2x + 5$$ . Then, $$\mathit{du} = 2\mathit{dx},$$ or $$\mathit{dx} = \frac{1}{2}\mathit{du}$$ . Making the appropriate substitutions, we have</paragraph>
        <paragraph math="block">$$\begin{array}{l} {{\int\sqrt{2x + 5}}\mathit{dx} = {\int{\sqrt{u}\left( {\frac{1}{2}\mathit{du}} \right) = \frac{1}{2}{\int{u^{1 \slash 2}\mathit{du}}}}}} \\ {\phantom{{\int\sqrt{2x + 5}}\mathit{dx}} = {\frac{1}{2}\left( \frac{2}{3} \right)u^{3 \slash 2} + C}} \\ {\phantom{{\int\sqrt{2x + 5}}\mathit{dx}} = {\frac{1}{3}\left( {2x + 5} \right)^{3 \slash 2} + C}} \end{array}$$</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="2" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Find $${\int\frac{x^{2}}{\left( {2x^{3} + 1} \right)^{3 \slash 2}}}\mathit{dx}$$ .</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>Let $$u = 2x^{3} + 1$$ , so that $$\mathit{du} = 6x^{2}\mathit{dx}$$ , or $$x^{2}\mathit{dx} = \frac{1}{6}\mathit{du}$$ . Making the appropriate substitutions, we have</paragraph>
        <paragraph math="block">$$\begin{array}{l} {{\int\frac{x^{2}}{\left( {2x^{3} + 1} \right)^{3 \slash 2}}}\mathit{dx} = {\int{\frac{\left( \frac{1}{6} \right)\mathit{du}}{u^{3 \slash 2}} = \frac{1}{6}{\int{u^{- 3 \slash 2}\mathit{du}}}}}} \\ {\phantom{{\int\frac{x^{2}}{\left( {2x^{3} + 1} \right)^{3 \slash 2}}}\mathit{dx}} = \left( \frac{1}{6} \right)\left( {- 2} \right)u^{- 1 \slash 2} + C} \\ {\phantom{{\int\frac{x^{2}}{\left( {2x^{3} + 1} \right)^{3 \slash 2}}}\mathit{dx}} = {- \frac{1}{3}\left( {2x^{3} + 1} \right)^{- 1 \slash 2} + C}} \\ {\phantom{{\int\frac{x^{2}}{{({2x^{3} + 1})}^{3 \slash 2}}}\mathit{dx}} = {- \frac{1}{3\sqrt{2x^{3} + 1}} + C}} \end{array}$$</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="3" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Find $${\int{xe^{2x^{2} - 1}}}\mathit{dx}$$ .</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>Let $$u = 2x^{2} - 1$$ , so that $$\mathit{du} = 4x\mathit{dx}$$ , or $$x\mathit{dx} = \frac{1}{4}\mathit{du}$$ . Then,</paragraph>
        <paragraph math="block">$$\begin{array}{l} {{\int{xe^{2x^{2} - 1}\mathit{dx}}} = {\frac{1}{4}{\int{e^{u}\mathit{du}}}}} \\ {\phantom{\int{xe^{2x^{2} - 1}\mathit{dx}}} = {\frac{1}{4}e^{u} + C}} \\ {\phantom{\int{xe^{2x^{2} - 1}\mathit{dx}}} = {\frac{1}{4}e^{2x^{2} - 1} + C}} \end{array}$$</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="4" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Automobile Pollution According to a joint study conducted by Oxnard’s Environmental Management Department and a state government agency, the concentration of carbon monoxide $$({CO})$$ in the air due to automobile exhaust is increasing at the rate given by</paragraph>
        <paragraph math="block">$$f(t) = \frac{8\left( {0.1t + 1} \right)}{300{({0.2t^{2} + 4t + 64})}^{1 \slash 3}}$$</paragraph>
        <paragraph>parts per million (ppm) per year $$t$$ . Currently, the $$CO$$ concentration due to automobile exhaust is $$0.16$$ ppm. Find an expression giving the $$CO$$ concentration $$t$$ years from now.</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>Let $$C(t)$$ denote the $$CO$$ concentration in the air due to automobile exhaust $$t$$ years from now. Then,</paragraph>
        <paragraph math="block">$$\begin{array}{l} {{C^{\prime}(t)} = {f(t)} = \frac{8\left( {0.1t + 1} \right)}{300\left( {0.2t^{2} + 4t + 64} \right)^{1 \slash 3}}} \\ {\phantom{{C^{\prime}(t)} = {f(t)}} = {\frac{8}{300}\left( {0.1t + 1} \right){({0.2t^{2} + 4t + 64})}^{- 1 \slash 3}}} \end{array}$$</paragraph>
        <paragraph>Integrating, we find</paragraph>
        <paragraph math="block">$$\begin{array}{l} {{C(t)} = {\int{\frac{8}{300}{({0.1t + 1})}{({0.2t^{2} + 4t + 64})}^{- 1 \slash 3}\mathit{dt}}}} \\ {\phantom{C{(t)}} = {\frac{8}{300}{\int\left( {0.1t + 1} \right){({0.2t^{2} + 4t + 64})}^{- 1 \slash 3}\mathit{dt}}}} \end{array}$$</paragraph>
        <paragraph>Let $$u = 0.2t^{2} + 4t + 64$$ , so that</paragraph>
        <paragraph math="block">$$\mathit{du} = \left( {0.4t + 4} \right)\mathit{dt} = 4\left( {0.1t + 1} \right)\mathit{dt}$$</paragraph>
        <paragraph>or</paragraph>
        <paragraph math="block">$$\left( {0.1t + 1} \right)\mathit{dt} = \frac{1}{4}\mathit{du}$$</paragraph>
        <paragraph>Then,</paragraph>
        <paragraph math="block">$$\begin{array}{l} {{C(t)} = {\frac{8}{300}\left( \frac{1}{4} \right){\int{u^{- 1 \slash 3}\mathit{du}}}}} \\ {\phantom{C(t)} = {\frac{1}{150}\left( {\frac{3}{2}u^{2 \slash 3}} \right) + k}} \\ {\phantom{C(t)} = {0.01{({0.2t^{2} + 4t + 64})}^{2 \slash 3} + k}} \end{array}$$</paragraph>
        <paragraph>where $$k$$ is an arbitrary constant. To determine the value of $$k$$ , we use the condition $$C(0) = 0.16$$ , obtaining</paragraph>
        <paragraph math="block">$$\begin{array}{rcl} {C{(0) = 0.16}} &amp; = &amp; {0.01(64)^{2 \slash 3} + k} \\ 0.16 &amp; = &amp; {0.16 + k} \\ k &amp; = &amp; 0 \end{array}$$</paragraph>
        <paragraph>Therefore,</paragraph>
        <paragraph math="block">$$C(t) = 0.01{({0.2t^{2} + 4t + 64})}^{2 \slash 3}$$</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
