    <exercise number="8" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Demand: Women’s Boots The rate of change of the unit price $$p$$ (in dollars) of Apex women’s boots is given by</paragraph>
        <paragraph math="block">$$p^{\prime}(x) = \frac{- 250x}{\left( {16 + x^{2}} \right)^{3 \slash 2}}$$</paragraph>
        <paragraph>where $$x$$ is the quantity demanded daily in units of a hundred. Find the demand function for these boots if the quantity demanded daily is $$300$$ pairs $$\left( {x = 3} \right)$$ when the unit price is $$\$ 50 \slash {pair}$$ .</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="9" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Population Growth The population of a certain city is projected to grow at the rate of</paragraph>
        <paragraph math="block">$${r(t) = 400\left( {1 + \frac{2t}{24 + t^{2}}} \right)} \left( {0 \leq t \leq 5} \right)$$</paragraph>
        <paragraph>people/year, $$t$$ years from now. The current population is $$60,000$$ . What will be the population $$5$$ years from now?</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$62,286$$</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="10" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Oil Spill In calm waters, the oil spilling from the ruptured hull of a grounded tanker forms an oil slick that is circular in shape. If the radius $$r$$ of the circle is increasing at the rate of</paragraph>
        <paragraph math="block">$$r^{\prime}(t) = \frac{30}{\sqrt{2t + 4}}$$</paragraph>
        <paragraph>ft/min $$t$$ min after the rupture occurs, find an expression for the radius at any time $$t$$ . How large is the polluted area $$16$$ min after the rupture occurred?</paragraph>
        <paragraph>Hint: $$r(0) = 0$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="11" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Life Expectancy of a Female Suppose that in a certain country, the life expectancy at birth of a female is changing at the rate of</paragraph>
        <paragraph math="block">$$g^{\prime}(t) = \frac{5.45218}{\left( {1 + 1.09t} \right)^{0.9}}$$</paragraph>
        <paragraph>years/year. Here, $$t$$ is measured in years, with $$t = 0$$ corresponding to the beginning of 1900. Find an expression $$g(t)$$ giving the life expectancy at birth (in years) of a female in that country if the life expectancy at the beginning of 1900 is $$50.02$$ years. What is the life expectancy at birth of a female born in 2000 in that country?</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$50.02(1 + 1.09t)^{0.1}$$ ; $$80$$</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="12" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Learning Curves The average student enrolled in the $$20$$ -week Court Reporting I course at the American Institute of Court Reporting progresses according to the rule</paragraph>
        <paragraph math="block">$${N^{\prime}(t) = 6e^{- 0.05t}} \left( {0 \leq t \leq 20} \right)$$</paragraph>
        <paragraph>where $$N^{\prime}(t)$$ measures the rate of change in the number of words per minute of dictation the student takes in machine shorthand after $$t$$ weeks in the course. Assuming that the average student enrolled in this course begins with a dictation speed of $$60{words} \slash \min$$ , find an expression $$N^{\prime}(t)$$ that gives the dictation speed of the student after $$t$$ weeks in the course.</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="13" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>U.S. Online Video Viewers The number of online video viewers in the United States was growing at the rate of</paragraph>
        <paragraph math="block">$$r(t) = 9.045e^{0.067t} (0 \leq t \leq 5)$$</paragraph>
        <paragraph>million viewers/year between 2008 $$(t = 0)$$ and 2013 $$(t = 5)$$ . The number of viewers stood at $$135$$ million in 2008.</paragraph>
        <list type="ordered">
          <item>Find an expression giving the number of online video viewers in year $$t$$ .</item>
          <item>How many viewers were there in 2012? In 2013? Source: eMarketer.</item>
        </list>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$135e^{0.067t}$$</paragraph>
        <paragraph>$$188.722$$ million</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="14" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Video Ads In recent years, video ads have become the most popular rich media format for ad buyers as advertisers have begun to realize returns on online video advertising. According to research and forecast by Forrester, media spending on ads for the years 2012 through 2017 is projected to grow at the rate of</paragraph>
        <paragraph math="block">$$R(t) = 0.538434e^{0.234t} (0 \leq t \leq 5)$$</paragraph>
        <paragraph>billion dollars/year in year $$t$$ , with $$t = 0$$ corresponding to 2012. The expenditure on media spending on ads in 2012 stood at $$\$ 2.9$$ billion.</paragraph>
        <list type="ordered">
          <item>Find an expression for media spending on ads in year $$t$$ .</item>
          <item>Assuming that the forecast was accurate, what would the media spending on ads be in 2016? Source: Forrester.</item>
        </list>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="15" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>U.S. Online Ad Revenues Online advertisement revenues took a slight dip in 2009, but since then, they have continued to grow spectacularly. In fact, the rate of growth of online advertisement revenues from 2009 $$(t = 0)$$ through 2012 $$(t = 3)$$ was approximately</paragraph>
        <paragraph math="block">$$r(t) = 3.1182e^{0.163(t + 1)} (0 \leq t \leq 3)$$</paragraph>
        <paragraph>billion dollars/year in year $$t$$ . Online advertisement revenues in 2009 were $$\$ 22.7$$ billion.</paragraph>
        <list type="ordered">
          <item>Find an expression for the online advertisement revenue in year $$t$$ .</item>
          <item>What were the online advertisement revenues in 2012? Source: IAB/PricewaterhouseCoopers.</item>
        </list>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$19.1301e^{0.613(t + 1)} + 3.5699$$</paragraph>
        <paragraph>$$\$ 40.3$$ billion</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="16" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Average Birth Height of Boys Using data collected at Kaiser Hospital, pediatricians estimate that the average height of male children changes at the rate of</paragraph>
        <paragraph math="block">$$h^{\prime}(t) = \frac{52.8706e^{- 0.3277t}}{\left( {1 + 2.449e^{- 0.3277t}} \right)^{2}}$$</paragraph>
        <paragraph>in./year, where the child’s height $$h(t)$$ is measured in inches and $$t$$ , the child’s age, is measured in years, with $$t = 0$$ corresponding to birth. Find an expression $$h(t)$$ for the average height of a boy at age $$t$$ if the height at birth of an average child is $$19.4$$ in. What is the height of an average 8-year-old boy?</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="17" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Amount of Glucose in the Bloodstream Suppose a patient is given a continuous intravenous infusion of glucose at a constant rate of $$r{mg} \slash \min$$ . Then, the rate at which the amount of glucose in the bloodstream is changing at time $$t$$ (in minutes) because of this infusion is given by</paragraph>
        <paragraph math="block">$$A^{\prime}(t) = re^{- at}$$</paragraph>
        <paragraph>mg/min, where $$a$$ is a positive constant associated with the rate at which excess glucose is eliminated from the bloodstream and is dependent on the patient’s metabolism rate. Derive an expression for the amount of glucose in the bloodstream at time $$t$$ .</paragraph>
        <paragraph>Hint: $$A(0) = 0$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$\frac{r}{a}\left( {1 - e^{- at}} \right)$$</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="18" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Concentration of a Drug In an Organ A drug is carried into an organ of volume $$V{cm}^{3}$$ by a liquid that enters the organ at the rate of $$a{{cm}^{3} \slash \sec}$$ and leaves it at the rate of $$b{{cm}^{3} \slash \sec}$$ . The concentration of the drug in the liquid entering the organ is $$c{g \slash {cm}^{3}}$$ . If the concentration of the drug in the organ at time $$t$$ (in seconds) is increasing at the rate of</paragraph>
        <paragraph math="block">$$x^{\prime}(t) = \frac{1}{V}\left( {ac - {bx}_{0}} \right)e^{- bt \slash V}$$</paragraph>
        <paragraph>$${g \slash {cm}}^{3} \slash \sec$$ and the concentration of the drug in the organ initially is $$x_{0}{g \slash {cm^{3}}}$$ , show that the concentration of the drug in the organ at time $$t$$ is given by</paragraph>
        <paragraph math="block">$$x(t) = \frac{\mathit{ac}}{b} + \left( {x_{0} - \frac{ac}{b}} \right)e^{- bt \slash V}$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
